Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
> On 15/08/10 23:00, Patric Mueller wrote:
>> Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
>> 
>>> software that
>>> heavily depends on just one person (development but mainly design) is
>>> not the most reliable basis for a long lasting development path that
>> 
>> On the other hand, obviously even a large team can't prevent that the
>> development stops.
>
> I don't think that this observation is helpful; the point is that
> "single-man-shows" usually don't pass the task to a successor.

Yes, but they don't have to if their repository is online and
preferably hosted somewhere else than their home machine.

Anybody can pick up UnNetHack or SporkHack at any revision the seem
fit and not only on the release code. Usually development of a
software project doesn't stop right after a release but the developer
works a bit more on the code.

Even if I drop off the planet tomorrow, anybody can use the
development version of UnNetHack as a starting point. Which is a big
plus as there have been lots of new features been added since the last
release in January. All that work would have been lost if I was
working behind closed curtains.


In the case of Vanilla NetHack we don't know how much work has been
done by the DevTeam. But looking at the buglist, it surely was
considerable work.

Now (and I think I can speak for Derek, too) I'd rather would have
started with the development version than with 3.4.3 with its more
than 400 known unfixed bugs (it's another question if we would have
started our own variants if a development version would exist).

This is quite infortunate as this leads to *duplicate work* and maybe
different bugs and *more work* when then next version finally will be
released.

> Tools live *and die* with changing interests or preferences of the
> single person. In a Devteam you have a fall-back. The history of
> the Nethack development shows that quite good; Nethack has a long
> history.

But you need a fallback that works :) To be fair, the NetHack DevTeam
had a really long time where it were active. AFAIK one of the longest
periods of an open source game.

An example where the development wasn't even always in the hands of
the same people is Crawl. After the single developer Linley retired a
group of people continued the development (that was a smooth
transition AFAIK) and after that group stalled, another single
developer carried on by itself before another group picked it up.

You don't necessarily need a DevTeam, you need people doing something.

>> [...]
>> 
>>> it more accurately who's involved in which tasks of the development
>>> and design process.) Folks get involved in their Real World tasks and
>>> we will have to expect a similar decline as in Nethack, probably in a
>>> even shorter time span in case of a smaller development/design team.
>> 
>> That might be true but both UnNetHack and SporkHack have the advantage
>> of a public repository. Even if Derek, Pasi and I get simultaneously
>> hit by lightning the code we worked on is still there.
>
> This is also no convincing argument, since the code is available; and
> in fact, even those mentioned variants are based on that code.

Sure, it could be worse. If the DevTeam only released binaries we
couldn't even do patches and variants.

But as I said above it's also quite unfortunate when the development
version diverges immensely from the released version.

The problem isn't confined to the code. Even if somebody would want to
fix all the bugs in vanilla NetHack 3.4.3, he couldn't do that easily
as the bug reports aren't available either and the bug descriptions
are sometimes vague beyond usability.

>> And not only the code of the last release version but also the
>> development code. That's an advantage of public development.
>
> This is true.
>
>> The DevTeam's way of doing development of an open source game is
>> antique.
>
> Maybe antique, maybe even inappropriate, maybe not.

I really have to think hard to come up with something good to say
about the DevTeam's "behind closed curtains" development model. 

Maybe the mystery or the surprises of a new release and its features?
But honestly, after 7 years the mystery tends to blur and fade.

The shortcomings are just to glaring if you compare it to development
in the Angband or Crawl community or any larger OSS project.

>> If we never see the next version all the work they have done will be
>> lost. And all the patches and bug reports that have been sent to them.
>
> <*shrug*>  If I were a Devteam member I'd bite my arm if I've had
> invested a lot time in bug-fixing (and probably also in new features)
> and get blocked by other contributors. I've no insights WRT Devteam's
> incentives.

It's still "we are working on it". That might be true from the
DevTeam's POV. Of course, only little of that work is visible.

It's also possible that it's a case of "people just can't let go".
I really would like to be proven wrong, though.

